  "id": "Compound::DB00818 - Gene::1571",
  "verbalization": "Propofol, a commonly used anesthetic agent, is shown to bind to Cyp2c18 and Cyp2b6, both of which are involved in the metabolism of arachidonic acid. These enzymes are part of the arachidonic acid epoxygenase and monooxygenase pathways, which are ultimately linked to Cyp2e1. The evidence suggests that Propofol may influence Cyp2e1 through the activation or modulation of these metabolic pathways. The high scores in the paths indicate a strong, consistent relationship between Propofol and Cyp2e1, with the shared involvement of Cyp2c18 and Cyp2b6 in arachidonic acid metabolism being a key mechanism. The presence of multiple independent pathways converging on Cyp2e1 supports the hypothesis that Propofol exerts a biological effect on Cyp2e1 via these metabolic routes. The mechanism is supported by the observed binding of Propofol to the enzyme and its role in modulating the activity of arachidonic acid metabolism, which is a well-documented pathway in the liver.",
